# Continuous colour scales: scale_colour_continuous

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

mpg = Giggly.Datasets.mpg()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`scale_colour_continuous()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/scale_colour_continuous.html),
which covers `scale_fill_continuous()`, `scale_colour_binned()` and `scale_fill_binned()` too, in
the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An
example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the page
is also a list of what remains.

`scale_colour_continuous()` and `scale_fill_continuous()` are the scales ggplot2 uses by default
when a column of numbers is mapped to `colour` or `fill`, blending smoothly through a palette;
`scale_colour_binned()` and `scale_fill_binned()` cut the numbers into bins and give each bin one
colour. Their default palette is a setting of the theme. Giggly has
`Giggly.Plot.scale_color_continuous/2` and `Giggly.Plot.scale_fill_continuous/2`, along the gradient
from `#132B43` to `#56B1F7` by default or through the colours `palette:` gives, but no binned scales
(`scale_colour_binned()`), and draws every plot in ggplot2's default theme, `theme_gray()`, with no
way to change one. A plot is described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with `Giggly.to_svg/2`, which
returns an SVG document. The setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells write `Plot.new/2`, and
defines `render`, which draws a plot and shows it below the cell with `Kino.Image`. The data in
these examples is `Giggly.Datasets.mpg/0`, ggplot2's `mpg`: fuel economy for 234 cars of 38 popular
models from 1999 to 2008, which the setup cell binds to `mpg`, ggplot2's name for it.

## A palette given to the scale

```r
# A standard plot
p <- ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy, colour = cty)) +
  geom_point()

# You can use the scale to give a palette directly
p + scale_colour_continuous(palette = c("#FEE0D2", "#FC9272", "#DE2D26"))
```

Given colours, the palette is a gradient through them, from the lowest `cty` to the highest.

```elixir
p =
  mpg
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :displ, y: :hwy, colour: :cty})
  |> Plot.geom_point()

p
|> Plot.scale_color_continuous(palette: ["#FEE0D2", "#FC9272", "#DE2D26"])
|> render.()
```

## A palette from the theme

```r
# The default colours are encoded into the theme
p + theme(palette.colour.continuous = c("#DEEBF7", "#9ECAE1", "#3182BD"))
```

Not in Giggly yet: changing the theme, here its `palette.colour.continuous` setting.

## A default for every plot

`update_theme()` changes the theme every later plot is drawn in, and returns the one it replaced.
Giggly has no global settings: every option is an argument.

```r
# You can globally set default colour palette via the theme
old <- update_theme(palette.colour.continuous = c("#E5F5E0", "#A1D99B", "#31A354"))

# Plot now shows new global default
p
```

Not in Giggly yet: changing the theme.

## Binned colours

```r
# The default binned colour scale uses the continuous palette
p + scale_colour_binned() +
  theme(palette.colour.continuous = c("#EFEDF5", "#BCBDDC", "#756BB1"))
```

Not in Giggly yet: `scale_colour_binned()` and changing the theme.

## Restoring the theme

```r
# Restoring the previous theme
theme_set(old)
```

`theme_set()` makes the theme saved in `old` the default again, undoing `update_theme()`. It draws
nothing. Giggly has no global settings, so there is nothing to restore.
